---
title: Ambient sounds
description: Edit an ambient sequence, or add a new ambient sound to your mod, and play it with an AMBIENT constant.
sidebar:
  order: 5
---

An ambient sound is a looping background sequence, such as wind or birdsong, that maps play with `PlayAmbientSounds`. Each one is an MSEQ file in `audio/mseq/`, and its `AMBIENT_` constant is named after the file, so `spooky.xml` becomes `AMBIENT_SPOOKY`.

## Editing an ambient sequence

To change how one of the game's ambient sounds plays, edit its MSEQ file. `AMBIENT_SPOOKY` plays `audio/mseq/spooky.xml`.

An MSEQ file has a header and a list of commands:

```xml
<Mseq code="501" firstVoice="13">
	<TrackRamps/>
	<Commands>
		<SetInstrument track="1" wav="crow"/>
		<SetVolume track="1" volume="7F"/>
		<PlaySound track="1" pitch="176" volume="6D"/>
		<Delay duration="10"/>
		<StopSound track="1" pitch="176"/>
	</Commands>
</Mseq>
```

- `SetInstrument` picks a sample by name for a track. The sample must be in an always-loaded bank, unless the sequence is one of the radio's stations.
- `PlaySound` and `StopSound` start and stop a note. `Delay` waits for `duration` ticks.
- `StartLoop` and `EndLoop` repeat the commands between them. An `EndLoop` with `count="0"` repeats forever, and the loop must contain a `Delay`.
- `SetVolume`, `SetPan`, and `SetReverb` set values for a track. `volume` is in hexadecimal.

The game's files in `audio/mseq/` show every command. Copy the one closest to what you want.

## Adding a new ambient sound

{% steps %}
1. In the Filesystem, copy one of the game's MSEQ files in `audio/mseq`, paste it into the same folder, and rename the copy after your sound, such as `rain.xml`.
2. Edit the copy. Set its `code` to a label of one to four characters, such as `RN1`.
3. Play the sound from a map's EVT script:

    ```c
    Call(PlayAmbientSounds, AMBIENT_RAIN)
    ```
{% /steps %}

## The radio

`audio/mseq/radio_0.xml` to `radio_3.xml` aren't ambient sounds of their own. They're the four stations of `AMBIENT_RADIO`. All four must play samples from the same bank, through their `SetInstrument` commands.

While the radio plays, the game loads its stations' bank, as it does for a song's banks, so the stations don't need an always-loaded bank. The game's stations play samples from `SPC3`. A bank that the radio loads isn't always loaded, so other ambient sounds and sound effects can't play its samples.
